          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you (as a sister).\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Jane directly avows a loving emotional state toward St John, explicitly qualifying it as sisterly rather than romantic. The avowal is presented as true and as one reason for her special trust in him.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal does not create, renew, or invoke an obligation binding Jane to marry St John or go to India. In the same turn she expressly denies any obligation and describes what she would have ventured with him hypothetically. Her earlier offer to accompany him as an assistant is a separate act, which he rejects because she will not be his wife; its undertaking force does not transfer to “I love you.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Yes, I will, as your assistant\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A female curate, who is not my wife, would never suit me.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is part of a controlled, reason-giving response in an argument over obligation and travel, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged exclamation. Its emotional content and tense situation do not by themselves establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.85,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing affection, the expression performs moderate relational clarification and boundary-setting: Jane positions her love as sisterly in the context of rejecting marriage, distinguishing familial or companionate attachment from the marital love St John’s proposal would require.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I say again, I will be your\\ncurate, if you like, but never your wife.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is defending herself against St John’s allegation that she is breaking a promise to join missionary work. She distinguishes her willingness to venture with him, grounded in admiration, trust, and sisterly affection, from any obligation to marry him, accompany strangers, or sacrifice her life by going to India.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The evidence supports genuine sisterly or friendly affection while excluding romantic attachment. Jane’s earlier “Yes, I will, as your assistant” is a separate proposed undertaking to accompany St John without marriage. St John refuses that arrangement as unsuitable, and Jane subsequently denies any binding promise or obligation. The focal love avowal explains her exceptional willingness toward him but does not itself undertake travel or marriage. St John gives no affirmative uptake to the love avowal and instead challenges her fear and attachment elsewhere.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre. The classification relies only on the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The passages strongly establish the sisterly, non-romantic framing and the immediate dispute over obligation. They do not independently settle every aspect of Jane’s longer-term intentions or whether her proposed missionary assistance could have become binding under different accepted terms.\"}"
        }
